From 945953ed03aaee685f10c02df93bb3afd2074d36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam James <sam@gentoo.org>
Date: Thu, 25 Jun 2026 13:40:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Drop G_GNUC_CONST for *_get_type

g_type_ensure has a large comment saying _get_type shouldn't be
marked with G_GNUC_CONST because it has side-effects on the first
run, but it's OK if you consistently call g_type_ensure first.

Unfortunately, this isn't accurate. GCC trunk (observed at r17-1725-ge259a182e1f100)
now analyses points-to information more effectively and propagates its
findings, so it sees the function modifies some global state, concludes
that can't be possible (because of the const attribute), and deletes
the stores.

pure unfortunately is unsuitable for *_get_type too as it has a side-effect
(writes to global memory).

Mesa had a similar issue which I fixed recently [0]. glib and glibmm likely
want the (not-yet-implemented) 'idempotent' attribute instead [1]. In fact,
this exact problem is what motivated that request! :)

ebassi mentions an alternative to the current setup as well, using
atomics [2].

This problem has gone back many years but the compiler has finally
fully outsmarted us here, so give in.

[0] https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/42361
[1] https://gcc.gnu.org/PR32911
[2] https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/merge_requests/4249#note_2583110

Fixes: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/work_items/3984
Signed-off-by: Sam James <sam@gentoo.org>
